# Server port — the main API listener (organization visibility in Choreo).
SERVER_PORT=8080

# Health port — a second, minimal listener carrying only GET /health and
# GET /health/database (internal/server/health.go). It is separate from
# SERVER_PORT because it is published at PUBLIC visibility so external
# alerting can poll it without credentials: keeping it on its own listener
# means no business route is reachable at that visibility, whatever a
# gateway rule says. Must not equal SERVER_PORT — Config.Validate rejects
# that at startup.
#
# Leave this at its default in a Choreo deployment: .choreo/component.yaml
# declares the public health endpoint against port 8081 statically, and
# nothing reconciles the two at deploy time — overriding it there routes
# public health traffic to a port nothing listens on, which reads to the
# alerting system as a permanent outage. Override it locally only.
HEALTH_PORT=8081

# Timeouts (Go duration strings). All optional; the values below are the
# defaults. Each must be > 0. Invalid values fail startup.
# SERVER_READ_TIMEOUT=60s
# SERVER_WRITE_TIMEOUT=60s
# REQUEST_TIMEOUT=60s
# UPSTREAM_CLIENT_TIMEOUT=60s

# Data source: "postgres" (default) or "servicenow"
DATA_SOURCE=postgres

# PostgreSQL — required when DATA_SOURCE=postgres. Optional for
# DATA_SOURCE=servicenow: db.NewPoolIfNeeded skips the pool so a local
# customer-portal can start without a reachable database.
#
# DB_USER/DB_PASSWORD/DB_NAME are all-or-nothing: set all three, or none.
# A partial set is rejected at startup, the same way the Event Hub group
# below is, so that a typo can't silently disable the endpoints listed next.
#
# With DATA_SOURCE=servicenow and no database configured, the service starts
# normally and serves every entity endpoint from the ServiceNow integration
# service. The Postgres-only side tables/endpoints (event_publish_failures,
# sla-status, scheduled_task_run) are simply not registered and their
# routes return 404:
#   - GET /sla-status...                              (reads the "sla" table)
#   - POST /event-publish-failures...                (event_publish_failures)
#   - scheduled_task_run endpoints
# A failed Event Hub publish is then logged rather than durably recorded, so
# configure a database if you rely on replaying failed publishes.
DB_HOST=localhost
DB_PORT=5432
DB_USER=<YOUR_DB_USER>
DB_PASSWORD=<YOUR_DB_PASSWORD>
DB_NAME=<YOUR_DB_NAME>
DB_SSLMODE=disable
# DB_SCHEMA pins the connection's search_path, in case the role's own default
# search_path would otherwise resolve to a different same-named schema, or to
# "public". Optional — left unset, it falls back to "DB_USER,public",
# matching Postgres' own default search_path verbatim (every deployment's
# tables live in "public" today, so this fallback must keep it). Same
# purpose as operations/csm-sync-service's own DB_SCHEMA.
# DB_SCHEMA=

# Pool tuning, all optional -- unset falls back to the values this file used
# to hardcode in internal/db/postgres.go (20/2/30m/5m).
# DB_POOL_MAX_CONNS=20
# DB_POOL_MIN_CONNS=2
# DB_POOL_MAX_CONN_LIFETIME=30m
# DB_POOL_MAX_CONN_IDLE_TIME=5m

# ServiceNow via Choreo — required when DATA_SOURCE=servicenow
SERVICENOW_INTEGRATION_SERVICE_BASE_URL=<SERVICENOW_INTEGRATION_SERVICE_BASE_URL>
SERVICENOW_INTEGRATION_SERVICE_TOKEN_URL=<SERVICENOW_INTEGRATION_SERVICE_TOKEN_URL>
SERVICENOW_INTEGRATION_SERVICE_CLIENT_ID=<SERVICENOW_INTEGRATION_SERVICE_CLIENT_ID>
SERVICENOW_INTEGRATION_SERVICE_CLIENT_SECRET=<SERVICENOW_INTEGRATION_SERVICE_CLIENT_SECRET>
SERVICENOW_INTEGRATION_SERVICE_SCOPES=<SERVICENOW_INTEGRATION_SERVICE_SCOPES>

# Product-consumption provisioning state (GET/PATCH /projects/{id}/consumption)
# — Postgres-backed, whatever the data source. These routes register wherever
# database credentials are configured, including DATA_SOURCE=servicenow
# deployments, because the flow mirrors its state into Postgres alongside
# ServiceNow. No configuration of their own is needed beyond the DB_* vars
# above.
#
# The state holds a project's Choreo OAuth2 credentials and its two
# subscription secret keys. These columns are shared with the ServiceNow sync,
# which writes them in the clear, and this service writes them the same way --
# it does not encrypt its own writes, because a second format in the same
# column could not be told apart from the sync's on read, and a row written
# here would stop matching the ServiceNow record the project row mirrors.
# Nothing reads the values back either: the read response reports only whether
# each is present. Encrypting them at rest is worth doing across every writer,
# which is a platform change rather than this service's to make.

# NOTE: CSM_TEAM_REGISTRY and CSM_USER_ROLES are no longer read by this
# service. The team registry and the assignable-role allow-list moved to the
# CSM portal backend and are resolved there at startup; see
# apps/csm-portal/backend/.env.example. Setting them here has no effect.

# Event Hub — internal/service.EventPublisherService publishes domain events
# (case.created/case.comment_added/case.status_changed/case.assigned/
# case.acknowledged/case.severity_changed/incident.created, the ServiceNow
# data source only) to Event Hub's Kafka-compatible endpoint for
# csm-notification-service to consume — see routes.go's EventPublisherService
# wiring.
# EVENT_HUB_BROKER/EVENT_HUB_CONNECTION_STRING/EVENT_HUB_TOPIC are
# all-or-nothing: leave all three unset, or set all three together, never
# just one or two — Config.Validate rejects a partial set at startup.
# EVENT_HUB_CONNECTION_STRING must be a namespace-level Shared Access Policy
# connection string (no EntityPath suffix) — an Event-Hub-scoped one will
# only authorize EVENT_HUB_TOPIC, not any other topic this service might
# produce to later.
EVENT_HUB_BROKER=
EVENT_HUB_CONNECTION_STRING=
# PROJECT_EVENT_HUB_TOPIC carries the onboarding events
# (project_contact.invited) instead of EVENT_HUB_TOPIC, so a backlog of case
# events cannot delay an invitation and the onboarding dead-letter queue can
# be watched on its own. Defaults to "project-events". Must match
# csm-notification-service's PROJECT_EVENT_HUB_TOPIC.
# PROJECT_EVENT_HUB_TOPIC=project-events
#
# --- Outage emails (event-driven) ---
# The internal-stakeholder notification and the SRE outage communication are
# decided about a second after any outage change (LISTEN on migration 0186's
# NOTIFY; OUTAGE_NOTICE_POLL_INTERVAL, default 60s, is only the fallback poll)
# and published on
# OUTAGE_EVENT_HUB_TOPIC (default "outage-events", must match
# csm-notification-service's) for csm-notification-service to send. Needs
# EVENT_HUB_BROKER + EVENT_PUBLISHING_ENABLED=true and a database.
# Each recipient list switches its email on; leave one empty and that email is
# not decided at all (nothing is marked sent that no one received).
# OUTAGE_EVENT_HUB_TOPIC=outage-events
#
# SRE_EVENT_HUB_TOPIC, when set, is the ONE topic both the change-request
# notices and the outage emails publish to, overriding CR_EVENT_HUB_TOPIC and
# OUTAGE_EVENT_HUB_TOPIC. csm-notification-service routes them by event type.
# Set the same SRE_EVENT_HUB_TOPIC there FIRST, then here.
# SRE_EVENT_HUB_TOPIC=sre-events
# OUTAGE_NOTICE_POLL_INTERVAL=60s
# OUTAGE_NOTIFICATION_RECIPIENTS=internal-stakeholders@wso2.com
# OUTAGE_COMMUNICATION_RECIPIENTS=sre-team@wso2.com
EVENT_HUB_TOPIC=
#
# EVENT_PUBLISHING_ENABLED is a separate switch on top of the three vars
# above — even with Event Hub fully configured, nothing is published until
# this is explicitly set to "true". Defaults to false (safe-by-default), so
# CreateCase/CreateIncident/etc. behave exactly as before this pipeline
# existed unless every one of EVENT_HUB_BROKER/_CONNECTION_STRING/_TOPIC AND
# this are all set.
EVENT_PUBLISHING_ENABLED=true
#
# --- Token validation (Asgardeo) --------------------------------------------
# Always on -- there is no flag to disable it. The three variables below are
# required at startup (the process exits without them, or if the JWKS isn't
# reachable), and a token that is present but invalid is rejected with 401 on
# every route.
AUTH_ISSUER=https://api.asgardeo.io/t/<org>/oauth2/token
AUTH_JWKS_URL=https://api.asgardeo.io/t/<org>/oauth2/jwks
# Comma-separated Asgardeo application client ids an x-user-id-token's aud must
# contain to be accepted as a user token.
AUTH_USER_TOKEN_AUDIENCES=<spa-client-id>
# AUTH_CLOCK_SKEW=30s
#
# Three separate configs classify a client-credentials caller (Authorization:
# Bearer's client_id/azp claim), in this priority order -- see
# internal/service/access_service.go's ResolveScope for the exact logic:
#
# 1. CUSTOMER_PORTAL_BACKEND_CLIENT_ID (singular): the client id of
#    apps/customer-portal/backend-v2 (or any successor). Checked FIRST and
#    ALWAYS resolved purely from the forwarded x-user-id-token -- never
#    unconditionally trusted, by construction, regardless of what else this
#    id might also be (mis)configured into (M2M_CLIENT_IDS, or equal to
#    CSM_PORTAL_BACKEND_CLIENT_ID by a copy-paste mistake: Config.Validate
#    rejects the latter at startup). This is what makes it structurally
#    impossible for a customer-facing BFF's client id to end up with
#    unconditional, RLS-bypassing access to every project and case for every
#    customer -- there is no shared list it could be accidentally added to.
CUSTOMER_PORTAL_BACKEND_CLIENT_ID=
#
# 2. CSM_PORTAL_BACKEND_CLIENT_ID (singular) + CSM_PORTAL_USER_DOMAIN: the
#    client id of apps/csm-portal/backend (the internal CS-engineer portal's
#    BFF). Unrestricted ONLY if the forwarded x-user-id-token's email also
#    ends in CSM_PORTAL_USER_DOMAIN (case-insensitive, e.g. "wso2.com", no
#    leading "@") -- trusting the client id alone is not enough, because this
#    caller always forwards a real human's request, and that human might not
#    actually be WSO2/partner staff (a misassigned Asgardeo role, for
#    instance). A caller using this client id whose email doesn't match the
#    domain is refused outright (403), not silently resolved some other way.
#    This also means a WSO2 engineer who hasn't been separately provisioned a
#    `user` table row is still never blocked: domain alone is enough, no
#    users-by-email lookup at all. Must be set together, or not at all --
#    Config.Validate rejects a partial pair at startup.
CSM_PORTAL_BACKEND_CLIENT_ID=
CSM_PORTAL_USER_DOMAIN=
#
# 3. M2M_CLIENT_IDS (plural, comma-separated): pure machine-to-machine
#    callers with no human in the loop at all (the GitHub webhook
#    delivery/service-request handlers, the Salesforce partner ingest, and
#    similar) -- unconditionally trusted with full access to every project
#    and case, regardless of any x-user-id-token it also carries (used only
#    for attribution, never for scoping). A caller matching none of the three
#    configs above is resolved purely from its x-user-id-token instead (an
#    INTERNAL user still sees everything; an EXTERNAL/customer user sees only
#    their own REGISTERED projects); with no user token and no match, the
#    request is refused (401).
# NEVER list a customer-facing BFF's client id here (e.g.
# apps/customer-portal/backend-v2) -- it belongs in
# CUSTOMER_PORTAL_BACKEND_CLIENT_ID above, which can never be unconditionally
# trusted even if also listed here by mistake.
# Deploy prerequisite: a service calling the scoped endpoints directly with only
# a client-credentials token (no x-user-id-token) gets a 401 unless its client
# id is listed here.
M2M_CLIENT_IDS=
#
# CUSTOMER_ROLES is a comma-separated list of ServiceNow role names whose
# presence on a case comment's resolved author marks that comment as a
# customer reply — moving the case back to "Waiting on WSO2" when it
# arrives while the case is "Awaiting Info"/"Solution Proposed" (see
# internal/service/sn_case_service.go's applyCustomerReplyStateTransition).
# Deliberately no committed default: this is organisation-specific
# vocabulary, not something to hardcode here. Left unset, that function
# simply can't confirm customer-authorship and skips (logged) — nothing
# else about comment creation is affected.
# CUSTOMER_ROLES=
#
# CS_ENGINEER_ROLE is the role name whose presence on a case comment's
# resolved author marks that comment as a qualifying CS-engineer/support-
# engineer response, completing the case's CSM-native "response" SLA clock
# (see internal/service/sn_case_service.go's applyResponseSLAOnComment,
# internal/service/case_service.go's completeResponseSLAOnComment, and
# internal/service/sla_engine_service.go). Also what sets the published
# case.comment_added event's own IsSupportEngineerResponse flag
# (isSupportEngineerAuthorSN / isSupportEngineerAuthor, both in the same two
# files) -- the signal integrations/csm-notification-service's own SLA
# tracking engine uses to complete a case's response clock on its side, with
# no role/identity resolution of its own. Shared by all of these -- the same
# role name is meaningful everywhere, since "CS engineer" and "support
# engineer" are the same real-world role, just checked via different
# lookups depending on data source. Deliberately no committed default, same
# reasoning as CUSTOMER_ROLES above. Left unset, every one of these simply
# can't confirm engineer-authorship and skips (logged).
# CS_ENGINEER_ROLE=
#
# SLA_RECOMPUTE_INTERVAL is how often the CSM-native SLA engine's
# background worker recomputes every source='CSM' "sla" row's elapsed
# percentage/breach status (internal/service/sla_engine_recompute_worker.go).
# Go duration string, e.g. "30s"/"1m". Defaults to 45s. Only runs at all
# when a database is configured (see HasDatabase).
# SLA_RECOMPUTE_INTERVAL=45s
#
# No CSM portal base URL config here: this service only publishes the fact
# that a case/incident was created — it does not build any portal link.
# incident.created's "Open in Portal" button target is built by
# csm-notification-service itself, from the event's own entity id, the same
# way it already builds case.created's portal link.

# REST sales/sales-entity-service — optional ingest for POST /salesforce/events
# (postgres only). Not GraphQL sales/entity-graphql-service. Not Salesforce REST.
# All-or-nothing: leave the four connection vars unset, or set all four together.
# Config.Validate rejects a partial set. The route is registered only when
# DATA_SOURCE=postgres, a pool is available, and all four are set (see routes.go).
# Auth is OAuth2 client_credentials, then POST {SALES_ENTITY_BASE_URL}/customer-search
# with {ids, isRealTime: true, limit: 1}. An empty array on CREATED/UPDATED/RESTORED
# is returned as 503 so the caller can retry (the ASB event can arrive before
# Salesforce commits).
# SALES_ENTITY_BASE_URL=
# SALES_ENTITY_TOKEN_URL=
# SALES_ENTITY_CLIENT_ID=
# SALES_ENTITY_CLIENT_SECRET=
# SALES_ENTITY_SCOPES=

# Customer onboarding: let POST /salesforce/events also act on Project_Contact__c
# and Contact envelopes (user / account_contact / project_contact upsert, DATABASE
# onboarding step, project_contact.invited event). Off by default: those
# envelopes are then acknowledged and ignored. Needs the SALES_ENTITY_* set above
# and the sf_id columns from the csm-sync migration 0076.
# CSM_MIGRATION_SALESFORCE_MEMBERSHIP_INGEST_ENABLED=true

# Delayed retry for memberships whose project or account was not in CSM yet when
# their event arrived (a new project reaches CSM through the ServiceNow sync up to
# five minutes later, after Service Bus has already dead-lettered the envelope).
# Every interval, re-runs DATABASE steps that FAILED with "project not found" /
# "account not found", are older than the interval, and have fewer than 12
# attempts. Go duration; defaults to 5m; "0" disables the job (so does an
# invalid or negative value, with a warning). Only runs when
# CSM_MIGRATION_SALESFORCE_MEMBERSHIP_INGEST_ENABLED=true.
# SALESFORCE_INGEST_RETRY_INTERVAL=5m

# Let POST /salesforce/events act on Account envelopes (account upsert and
# soft-delete). Off by default, and keep it off while the ServiceNow sync still
# writes the account table: the two would fight over the same rows.
# CSM_MIGRATION_SALESFORCE_ACCOUNT_INGEST_ENABLED=true

# Let POST /salesforce/events act on Opportunity envelopes: upsert sf_opportunity by
# sf_id, replace its sf_opportunity_product line items from the same Sales Entity
# response, hard-delete on DELETED; Invoice__c envelopes likewise upsert sf_invoice by
# sf_id and OpportunityLineItem envelopes upsert one sf_opportunity_product row (the
# opportunity is ingested first when missing). Off by default, and keep it off while
# csm-sync-service still copies these tables from ServiceNow (both on means duplicate
# rows). The parent account must be in CSM, or CSM_MIGRATION_SALESFORCE_ACCOUNT_INGEST_ENABLED
# must be on so the account is ingested first. The same flag covers Linked_Opportunity__c
# envelopes (sf_opportunity_link), which also need their project in CSM (see the project
# switches below).
# CSM_MIGRATION_SALESFORCE_OPPORTUNITY_INGEST_ENABLED=true

# Let POST /salesforce/events act on Project__c envelopes: update the ten
# Salesforce-owned project columns (resolved by sf_id, then key), soft-mark
# is_active = FALSE on DELETED. Off by default. Update-only unless the insert
# switch below is on too: a project CSM does not have fails with "project not
# found" and the delayed-retry job re-runs it once ServiceNow has synced the row.
# CSM_MIGRATION_SALESFORCE_PROJECT_INGEST_ENABLED=true
# Let the Project ingest (and the membership / linked-opportunity ingests, via
# EnsureProject) insert projects CSM does not have. Keep it off while
# csm-sync-service still inserts project rows (project.key is UNIQUE, so an
# ingest-created row makes its insert fail forever); turn it on at cutover.
# CSM_MIGRATION_SALESFORCE_PROJECT_INSERT_ENABLED=true
# Refresh an account's stored partner links (account_relationship "Is Partner Of" /
# "Is Customer Of") from Salesforce after each Account event and each partner-contact
# membership event, and register POST /salesforce/accounts/{sfId}/refresh-partners.
# Off by default; keep it off while csm-sync-service still copies account_relationship.
# CSM_MIGRATION_SALESFORCE_PARTNER_INGEST_ENABLED=true

# Customer onboarding H-0: let POST /users/me/memberships/register mark the signed-in
# user's still-INVITED memberships as REGISTERED in Salesforce (clear the contact
# lockout flag, then set the membership state, then re-ingest). Off by default —
# must be exactly "true"; while off the route is not registered at all (404) and
# nothing on this path can write to Salesforce. Needs the SALES_ENTITY_* set above
# and CSM_MIGRATION_SALESFORCE_MEMBERSHIP_INGEST_ENABLED=true (the re-ingest goes through it).
# CSM_MIGRATION_MEMBERSHIP_REGISTRATION_ENABLED=true

# Customer onboarding, write side: register the portal-driven membership writes
# (POST/PATCH/DELETE /projects/{id}/contacts[/{email}] and .../resend-invitation).
# Both portals invite, re-role and deactivate customer users through them, and each
# write updates Postgres and Salesforce together or neither. Off by default (the
# value must be exactly "true"), and with it off the routes are not registered at
# all rather than answering 403. Needs DATA_SOURCE=postgres, a database, the
# SALES_ENTITY_* set above, and the Sales Entity create endpoints
# (POST /contacts, POST /project-contacts) to be deployed.
# CSM_MIGRATION_PORTAL_WRITES_ENABLED=true

# Allocation-app events into customer engagements (POST /customer-engagements/allocation-events).
# Off by default; needs a Postgres-authoritative DATA_SOURCE. The Firefighting type sys_id is per
# environment; unset skips creating firefighting engagements.
# CSM_MIGRATION_CUSTOMER_ENGAGEMENT_INGEST_ENABLED=true
# CUSTOMER_ENGAGEMENT_FIREFIGHTING_TYPE_ID=

# Cloud status: which outages drive the public status dashboard.
#
# The parent SERVICES whose service offerings are in scope, comma-separated.
# This is the Postgres form of the hardcoded list in the ServiceNow flow's
# trigger condition, and the filter it produces is
#     outage.service_offering_id -> service_offering.parent_id IN (these)
# so it matches on the OFFERING'S PARENT, never on outage.service_id. An
# outage whose CI is a service directly cannot match, which is what
# ServiceNow does.
#
# Fourteen today, covering asgardeo, choreo, bijira, devant and moesif.
# choreo-eu and agent-manager are deliberately absent: they are fully built
# out in the dashboard API but appear in neither the ServiceNow trigger nor
# its webhook URL script, and adding them here would make the port do
# something ServiceNow never did. Decide that deliberately.
#
# These are dev values -- read the production flow's trigger before
# configuring production.
# CLOUD_STATUS_SERVICE_IDS=492c649b-471e-f510-a0a2-9cd3846d43e3,40ae701b-479e-f510-a0a2-9cd3846d43cb

# How often the outbox drainer looks for outage changes. Default 10s.
# CLOUD_STATUS_POLL_INTERVAL=10s

# Turns on the background drainer, which records outage transitions AND
# rewrites cloud_monitor.status. OFF by default, and separate from the scope
# above on purpose.
#
# *** THE STATUS WRITE IS WHY THIS HAS ITS OWN SWITCH. *** While
# csm-sync-service's one-time bulk migration is still running, both it and
# this drainer can write cloud_monitor.status. Clearing
# CLOUD_STATUS_SERVICE_IDS would stop the drainer but also disable the sweep
# endpoint and the dashboard reads, so the write needs a switch that does not
# take everything else with it.
# CLOUD_STATUS_DRAINER_ENABLED=true

# --- Incident report flows ---------------------------------------------------
# Ports the ServiceNow flows "Create Incident Report Task" (incident state ->
# In Progress creates an incident_task of type Incident Report) and "Incident
# Report Generator" (state -> Resolved writes the report template into
# incident.incident_report). Always runs when there is a database -- no switch,
# like the ServiceNow flows. Writes Postgres only; ServiceNow's own flows keep
# writing ServiceNow.
#
# How often the drainer looks for incident state changes. Default 5s.
# INCIDENT_REPORT_POLL_INTERVAL=5s

# --- User cache (Redis) -------------------------------------------------------
# Optional Redis cache in front of GET /users/{id} and GET /users/me
# (internal/cache, wired in routes.go). Only used with a database
# (DATA_SOURCE=postgres); leave both REDIS_URL and REDIS_ADDR unset and every
# read goes to Postgres, exactly as before. Same convention as
# integrations/csm-notification-service, and the same Choreo Redis can be used.
#
# REDIS_URL is the managed (Azure Managed Redis, TLS-only) form and wins when
# both are set: rediss://:<access-key>@<host>:<port>. It carries the access key,
# so set it as a SECRET in Choreo -- never commit a real value here. Startup
# rejects a URL whose scheme is not redis:// or rediss://, without echoing it.
# The target must be non-clustered or use the "Enterprise" clustering policy,
# not "OSS Cluster".
# REDIS_URL=rediss://:<YOUR_REDIS_ACCESS_KEY>@<host>:10000
#
# REDIS_ADDR/REDIS_PASSWORD are the plain, non-TLS pair for a local Redis,
# e.g. `docker run -p 6379:6379 redis:7`.
# REDIS_ADDR=localhost:6379
# REDIS_PASSWORD=
#
# A Redis outage never fails a request: reads fall through to Postgres and a
# warning is logged at most every 30s. Every writer of user/contact/membership
# data deletes the affected user's entries after it commits, so USER_CACHE_TTL
# is only the backstop for a missed invalidation. Go duration; defaults to 10m
# (an unparseable or non-positive value also means 10m).
# USER_CACHE_TTL=10m
